 have  little reason to expect  swift change  and we have ophelia harms from dw's  latin america  desk with us good morning affiliate  thanks for joining us we now know that  nicolas maduro's  party has as expected won control of the  national assembly  what does this mean yeah no real  surprises here sumi  what it means is that he will continue  to tighten his grip of power  he has already ruled the country with an  authoritarian regime but now  he gains the last institution that was  in control of the  of the opposition with this institution  he gets the legislative power  and that will only mean that he will  continue  to uh strengthen his his power  um in in real practical terms it doesn't  change much in the country though  the bad news for the maduro regime is  that the economic situation will  probably continue because he will  continue to be isolated from the  international community  what about the opposition here we saw  images in the report of juan guay though  you know the opposition leader there was  so much energy around him but  it seems like they're now in a very  difficult position so where do you think  this leaves them  well um this election has shown that  they are divided again as they have been  in the past and  waido has been losing power and strength  in the last  two two years we haven't seen him very  active  some people say he might have needed  more um support from the international  community but the the  problem now is that he will have to step  down he and his parliament  even if he tries to continue  administrative powers as he has said he  will do in a referendum he will have to  step down  and he might even face persecution and  will have to end up in exile as many  other  opposition leaders the opposition will  have to  regain power together they are divided  and they will have to find  a new leader that might um help them  you mentioned the economic crisis and  the humanitarian situation also in  venezuela right now is is dire i mean  how did venezuelans see this election  well the low participation that we have  seen so far shows that  people really didn't have much interest  in this election not only because the  outcome was clear  to everybody but because people are very  much more worried about how to get food  and how to get every day's needs that  are really um  a struggle in day-to-day lives for  venezuelans  um what we see now is that the economic  situation won't change much the  the maduro regime doesn't have a real  clear strategy on how to tackle the  crisis  and so uh the situation for venezuelans  won't change much we might see even more  venezuelans  fleeing the country joining those five  million migrants that already  have fled uh venezuela and what about  the international community here we know  that the u.s  also called this election a fraud that  there were no international observers  from the european union for example  um what role does the international  community and  its view on this election play well  they didn't send any international  observers because there are no real  signs that there's any transparency the  the electoral body is controlled by the  maduro regime the  the judiciary is controlled by them as  well  but it is important that the  international community  doesn't support them because this will  continue to  to strengthen the the sanctions that  will  continue to affect venezuela and we can  throw  a bigger analogy worldwide you can see  that  the liberal democracies haven't really  found a way to deal with the  authoritarian's regime  as a venezuelan and this can apply to  many other countries as well  ophelia hamzaruthi from dws latin  america desk thank you so much for your  analysis
 

